---
// Lesson 11.4a — MC Strategy + Practice Set 1
// assessment: MC format, time budget, elimination strategy, set 1.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.4a — MC Strategy + Practice Set 1</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    70 questions, 120 minutes — about 100 seconds each, no penalty
    for guessing. Today is not about learning new content; it is
    about a STRATEGY for a 2-hour sprint, plus your first
    full-conditions practice set. Read the
    <a href="../exam/mc-strategy.html">strategy guide</a> first.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Strategy</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The three-pass method:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Pass 1 (fast, ~60 min):</strong> answer every
            question you know instantly. Flag the rest — NO
            lingering. A skipped question costs 100 seconds; a
            lingered one costs 300 and steals the same points
            from later questions</li>
        <li><strong>Pass 2 (~40 min):</strong> the flagged ones
            you can eliminate on. Wrong answers have TELLS —
            read them before solving</li>
        <li><strong>Pass 3 (~20 min):</strong> what remains.
            Answer EVERYTHING. No penalty = guessing is free
            points; an empty bubble is the only wrong choice</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Wrong-answer tells you already know from this
    class:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Off-by-one options (loop runs n vs n-1 times — the
            exam builds distractors from off-by-one
            mistakes)</li>
        <li>"Always/never" absolutes (rarely true — the world
            runs on tradeoffs, <a href="9.3.html">9.3</a>)</li>
        <li>Right answer with wrong REASON (two options say
            "10"; only one says why) — the "why" options test
            whether you traced the code or guessed the number</li>
        <li>Binary/hex conversion options with transposed digits
            (<a href="9.2.html">9.2</a>'s 0x1F vs 0xF1 — they
            KNEW that was your error)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The MC section includes some questions about
            "innovations' impacts" (IOC-1) with no code in them.
            Where does the time you save by bubbling fast on
            tracing questions go — and why is a 100-second
            average a LIE (some questions need 30s, some need
            180)?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity assessment">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Practice Set 1</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Set 1: 15 questions, 15 minutes, timed
    strictly.</strong> Your teacher hands out Set 1 (or posts it) —
    a mix across the CED families. Track per question:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Family (data? algorithms? networks? systems? impact?)</li>
        <li>Your time: instant / eliminator / guess</li>
        <li>Right or wrong</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>The scorecard matters more than the score.</strong>
    Grade, then build your error table: which FAMILY bleeds
    points? Your answer decides your homework — and the next two
    classes target it (<a href="11.4b.html">11.4b</a> data and
    networks, <a href="11.4c.html">11.4c</a> algorithms and
    systems).</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Strategy bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You got 11/15 — and spent 4 minutes on one
                question you still missed. What does the math
                say about that trade (4 minutes = 2.4 easy
                questions elsewhere)?</li>
            <li>You changed 3 answers at the end, 2 were wrong
                changes. What does research say about first
                instincts (they beat panicked revisions — when
                do you change: only with a REASON found
                afterward)?</li>
            <li>Your error table is all one family. What is
                tonight's study plan — and why is "study
                everything" the wrong plan this close to the
                exam?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the three-pass method and the 100-second average's lie</li>
            <li>Name three wrong-answer tells and spot them in Set 1</li>
            <li>Show your error table with families and times</li>
            <li>Have a study plan targeting your weakest family</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/mc-strategy.html">MC Strategy</a> — the full guide</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/reference-sheet.html">Exam Reference Sheet</a> — keep it beside every set</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/coverage.html">CED Coverage Map</a> — where your weak family lives in this course</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.4b.html">11.4b — MC Practice 2: Data &amp; Networks</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.4a" title="11.4a MC Strategy + Practice Set 1 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
